        "She almost dismisses you and then does not. The almost is a hand. \"Ask,\" she says. \"You get three questions that are not about whether I like you. I like you. Liking is not the job. The job is whether I can still see you in your eyes when a senator is done. Ask about the work.\"\n\nYou can ask about the kill-switch, about Crowe's next tape, about whether Revelations will sell you if the buyer is pretty enough. You pick the ones that will still matter when the dress arrives. She answers some. She refuses one with a look that is almost sorry. The refusal is information: there is a floor she will not put you on yet. Yet is a word that has buried people, and also the only word that has saved them. You do not spend your three questions on whether she will still like you after Ives. You already have that answer. Liking is not the job.",
        "\"Last thing.\" She steps in, not to touch, to be heard under the rain. \"If you are honest, do not become stupid with loyalty. If you lied, do not become stupid with pride. If you are double, do not become stupid with cleverness. Stupid is the only sin I still believe in. The rest is weather.\" Her breath is rye and rain. You could kiss her. The thought is a flare. She sees it and does not take it, and the not-taking is the most intimate thing left on the deck.\n\n\"Go,\" she says. \"If you look back I will be here, because I am theatrical when I am sad. Do not give me the scene.\" You go. You do not look back until the tram doors, and then you look, because you are theatrical when you are afraid, and she is still under the leaking ad, cupless, subtracting, a woman who thinks she can pull you out. You hold that thought in your mouth like the word you did not say. The doors close. The deck becomes a painting. The painting is already in a file.",
