        "The painting holds for one more stop of the eyes through wet glass. Voss does not wave. Waving would be a postcard. She turns her collar up and becomes a woman waiting for weather, which is the oldest cover she owns. You become a person on a tram, which is yours. Between the two covers the hour sits: honest, lie, or double, a coal, a thinner book or a thicker one, a palm that counted or a coat that stayed shut.\n\nA man of twenty-two with a delivery crate, adult, swearing at a wet label, blocks the door and then does not. You take a strap. The vibration is a known devil. You do not come. You do not cry. You ride the first meters of the tube as if the tube were a throat that could still choose not to swallow you. Rain on the glass turns the neon into a wound that refuses to clot.",
        "You understand, with a clarity that is almost rude, that she will send you anyway. That was the rail before you opened your mouth. The fork was never the gala. The fork was which woman you will be standing in front of when the music ends: the one who heard you, the one you pretty-foldered, or the one you split yourself to keep. All three will put you in a dress. All three will put a van two plates over. The difference is the temperature of the soup and whether extraction is a plan or a rumor.\n\nKane will want a note. You do not write it yet. Writing it on a tram is how notes become photographs. You put your forehead against the rail for a second, a civilian gesture, and then you stand like an operator again. Plate Nine falls behind like a bad decision you are going to keep. The gala is already walking toward you in a garment bag. Mia is a door you have not opened. The body Vale wrote rides with you, loud, employed, still answering to {{name}}.",
