        "The tram home is the same machine as this morning and a different country. You hold the rail. You do not look for toner. You replay the fork: honest, lie, double. Each one has a taste. Honest tastes like rye and a thinner skin. Lie tastes like a pretty empty folder and a colder van. Double tastes like copper and a third flask. You swallow the one you chose until it becomes part of the rewrite, another graft, this one in the part of you that still talks to handlers.\n\nA woman of about thirty-five sleeps against the glass, mouth open, civilian, unassigned. You envy the assignment. You do not wake her. Waking people is a firm habit. You are off the clock in the only way that is allowed: between plates, in motion, unphotographable as a decision. The sleeping woman's mouth stays open. You envy the assignment until the next brake reminds your pelvis that envy is not cover.",
        "Your phone stays dark. Kane will want a note. Crowe will want a pulse. Lyle will not write. Ren will not write. Mia's joke waits. You open none of it. The rain on the windows writes enough. You think about the dress arriving tomorrow like a verdict. You think about Ives's daughter, who is not in this story except as a wound a man spends. You think about Voss's boat that was real and country that was not. You decide, a small private decision, that if you get a boat you will not send a postcard. You will send a door. You do not know how to build one yet. Not knowing is not the same as not intending.\n\nPlate Four opens. You step into drizzle that has been turned down for the night shift. The preacher is gone. The kiosk doors are still three. You do not go in yet. You stand in the yellow band of the platform as if the day might offer a second thesis. It does not. It offers wet concrete and the walk home.",
