        "Inside, the follow-up card is still on the table. You put Voss's hour on top of it, invisible, a heavier paper. The cheap rectangle waits. You do not give it a second meeting. Once was a crisis. Twice, tonight, would be a ritual, and she told you not to give anyone the scene.\n\nYou eat something that requires a fork. You think about soup and do not smile. You wash the plate because washing is civilian and you are collecting civilian acts like ammunition. The ventilation throat breathes. The paper flower holds. You tell it she listened, or she did not, depending on the knife you picked, and that you are going to a gala anyway, and that anyway is the spine of the job.",
        "In bed the body asks for a hand. You give it one or you give it sleep; either way you do not call it aftercare. The word is ruined. The sensation is not. If you come, you come as {{name}}, in a slot, adult, un-audited, a small theft. If you do not, the hum stays, a reminder that unused charge is a tradecraft problem you will solve without a wafer. Sleep, when it comes, is not Vale's briefings. It is a van two plates over, a driver who hates someone, a kit that can open a door. You are not in the van. You are the reason the van exists. That is a kind of love that does not get to say its name.\n\nToward dawn the grid lifts the rain to industry again. You are still here. She is still thinking she can pull you out. Crowe is still thinking he already has. The gala does not care what either of them thinks. The gala cares whether you arrive in the dress. You will. That was never the question. The question is who you will be standing in front of when the music ends, and whether you will still flinch when they use your name like a key. Kane wanted the flinch. You are beginning to want it too. Wanting your own flinch is the first piece of a door.",
