        "You wake before the civic anthem and lie still long enough to remember which fork you put in her book. Honest. Lied. Double. The word is a coal. You hold it. You do not spit it. Spitting would be a scene for a grate-flower that has already done its work.\n\nTomorrow — today, soon — a dress will arrive with no note. You will wear it. You will decide about Mia. You will walk into Helix light and Ives's hand and Marek's corridor and you will price what you can. Voss will be in a van. Vale will be in a wing. Crowe will be on a balcony, counting. You will be in a body that has been written on and still, inconveniently, insists on being a person.\n\nThe anthem leaks through the wall, tinny, civic, almost kind. You stand. You wash. You do not look long at the glass. The day of the civilian visit is starting, and after that the night of the firm. You put the pistol in the bag that looks like groceries. You put {{name}} in your mouth. You step out. The rain has been decided. So has the gala. What has not been decided is the last page, and you are still, against policy, writing it.",
